{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And Timna was a concubine.</b> In Chronicles it implies that Timna was a male; therefore one must say there were two Timnas — one male and one female. As we find in Chronicles, 'and Timna' — the Timna here was the sister of Lotan, while the Timna of Chronicles is a male, and he is a son of Eliphaz. Nevertheless, the Timna here refers to both of them, and therefore there is a paseq between <b>and Timna</b> and between <b>was</b>. And this is what it says: the sons of Eliphaz [were] Teman, Omar, Zepho, and Gatam, and Kenaz; <b>and Timna</b> — the female — was a concubine to Eliphaz. And a parallel to this is written in the book of Joshua: 'And now, apportion this land as an inheritance to the nine tribes and the half-tribe of Menasseh'; 'the Reubenites and the Gadites took their inheritance, etc.' — and one must say that 'the half-tribe of Menasseh' refers to what is before it and after it." "<b>And Timna was a concubine to Eliphaz.</b> Rashi: he did not count her among the children of Seir, because she was their sister from the mother but not from the father — implying that if she had been their sister from the father, he would have counted them. חז״ק. And see in the portion וירא regarding 'my sister, daughter of my father' what Rashi explained: 'a daughter of one's father is permitted to the children of Noaḥ,' etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:33.112624922Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}